NAME
    walk - walk a directory tree without recursion
           (#include "../lib_walk.c")

SYNOPSIS
    #include "../lib_walk.c"      (includes lib_apath.c and lib_fs.c)
    if (!walk_open(dir)) { ... not a directory ... }
    while (walk_next()) { ... went, wname, wlev, wpath, wdown ... }

DESCRIPTION
    The tree walker of tree, find, dir -R, grep -r and cp -r.
    walk_open(path)  start at a directory (relative or absolute; "" is
                     the current one); 1 ok, 0 not a directory
    walk_next()      the next entry, depth first in directory order,
                     "." and ".." skipped; 1 with
                         went    the 32-byte entry
                         wname   its name
                         wlev    its depth (0 = in the start directory)
                         wpath   the directory holding it (absolute)
                     0 when the walk is over
    wdown            1 after walk_next returned a directory, which the
                     NEXT walk_next enters; clear it to skip that
                     subtree
    walk_full(out)   out <- wpath + "/" + wname (WFLEN = 144 bytes)
    walk_close()     stop early (closes the directory handle)
    wtrunc           1 when a directory was not entered because WMAX
                     (8) levels or the APLEN (128) path bytes ran out

    An explicit level stack does the work of the recursion y1cc
    rejects. Each level keeps the length of wpath and how many entries
    of its directory were read; ONE directory handle is open at a time:
    entering a subdirectory closes the parent's handle, and leaving it
    reopens the parent by path and skips the entries already read. So a
    walk costs one of the four handles, and a command can open files
    (grep -r) or create them (cp -r) along the way. The order is plain
    pre-order: a directory's contents follow it directly.

EXAMPLES
    walk_open(argstr());                    tree, in short
    while (walk_next()) {
        for (i = 0; i < wlev; i++) putstr("  ");
        putstr(wname); if (wdown) putchar('/'); putchar(10);
    }

NOTES
    New in Y1/OS 2026-09-23. It replaces the recursive walk(),
    collect() and copy_tree() of the P8X tree, find, dir, grep and cp,
    which also had to record each level's entries before descending
    because the P8X BIOS FNEXT cursor was global.

SEE ALSO
    tree, find, dir, grep, cp, apath
